01:07:46.645 --> 01:07:50.085
That fund currently has about $180,000

01:07:50.085 --> 01:07:51.925
in it, something in that range.

01:07:52.485 --> 01:07:56.565
I believe the board of education is concerned that

01:07:56.725 --> 01:07:59.125
they have a student currently

01:07:59.445 --> 01:08:01.925
that is likely to be out placed,

01:08:02.540 --> 01:08:06.780
is either already out placed now or is likely to be out placed

01:08:07.180 --> 01:08:08.220
in the future,

01:08:08.700 --> 01:08:09.980
in the short term,

01:08:10.140 --> 01:08:12.620
and that that's likely to cost the district,

01:08:12.940 --> 01:08:14.460
you know, a $100,000

01:08:14.460 --> 01:08:18.765
a year or so, and that's not an expense they budgeted

01:08:18.765 --> 01:08:20.045
up to this point.

01:08:22.285 --> 01:08:25.885
So is that coming out of the 149,000?

01:08:26.445 --> 01:08:28.605
>> No, that is coming out of

01:08:29.580 --> 01:08:32.780
the current budget that we're operating under

01:08:33.500 --> 01:08:35.340
indicating that they will

01:08:36.620 --> 01:08:39.660
they will underspend their budget by

01:08:39.740 --> 01:08:45.975
more than 2%. And therefore they're asking for permission to put 2% of that into their

01:08:46.935 --> 01:08:48.135
essentially contingency

01:08:48.135 --> 01:08:49.895
>> fund. Luis,

01:08:49.895 --> 01:08:53.415
you may remember a few meetings ago, we had a request

01:08:54.375 --> 01:09:04.890
to do that for last year's budget, which we declined to act on. I think there's about 82,000 that should be coming back to the town based on that. This would be the

01:09:05.210 --> 01:09:07.290
analog for this fiscal year.

01:09:07.690 --> 01:09:13.530
And I don't know that there is any kind of deadline for that. Mean, well, there is a deadline, but I don't think it's until the fall

01:09:13.985 --> 01:09:20.705
>> or or the bid year or something like that. That's correct, Mark. The the the year has to close out in July.

01:09:21.025 --> 01:09:22.385
We'll then do final

01:09:22.705 --> 01:09:28.785
closeouts through the month of August, and then the they would then submit that they wanna try to keep the funds at that point. Yeah.

01:09:29.830 --> 01:09:30.470
But

01:09:32.230 --> 01:09:37.670
I I'm a little surprised that they're asking for the well, they've got $1.14

01:09:37.670 --> 01:09:39.190
$9.09

01:09:39.190 --> 01:09:45.105
13 excess. They're asking for 2% of that which would be 81,300,

01:09:45.585 --> 01:09:46.465
but

01:09:46.785 --> 01:09:51.745
it doesn't make sense given that they already have a 180,000

01:09:51.745 --> 01:09:53.105
in that account.

01:09:54.945 --> 01:09:55.905
>> Yeah and

01:09:56.660 --> 01:10:04.660
I'll say that my view of this request is that it's just a heads up. You know, we don't have to act on it tonight. We don't have to make any

01:10:04.900 --> 01:10:07.780
decisions. This is just something that they're

01:10:08.500 --> 01:10:11.860
giving us, you know, basically a heads up that this is going to occur.

01:10:12.435 --> 01:10:22.115
I'm sure they'd like a commitment on it. I think that would be premature and unwise on the part of the Board of Finance to go down that road when we don't really know what the future holds in the next three months, much less

01:10:22.355 --> 01:10:29.010
six or eight, but that's kind of my position. I do not think we need to make any determination on that right now.

01:10:35.810 --> 01:10:38.370
Any other questions or comments on that?

01:10:46.295 --> 01:10:47.175
All right.

01:10:47.735 --> 01:10:50.135
So it seems to me that,

01:10:50.855 --> 01:10:51.815
I'll

01:10:51.815 --> 01:10:54.055
say that until we really have

01:10:55.220 --> 01:10:56.340
better information,

01:10:56.740 --> 01:10:58.500
there's not much more that we can really

01:11:00.740 --> 01:11:04.740
do in terms of the school budget. It's just a component of our overall budget.

01:11:05.380 --> 01:11:09.220
I think we've got an idea of where they've gone. They did make an effort and they reduced

01:11:09.555 --> 01:11:10.915
almost 150,000

01:11:10.915 --> 01:11:12.035
from

01:11:12.515 --> 01:11:13.715
their last budget.

01:11:14.915 --> 01:11:21.715
It's just gonna be a roll up component and topic of discussion I'm sure for next week assuming we get the details in time.

01:11:27.140 --> 01:11:29.620
Anybody have any other questions or comments on that?

01:11:34.260 --> 01:11:35.060
Okay,

01:11:35.540 --> 01:11:36.180
hearing none.

01:11:37.455 --> 01:11:44.895
I guess we can move to item 8c which is the revised fiscal year twenty twenty one proposed budget from the board of selectmen.

01:11:45.615 --> 01:11:46.895
This is the one that

01:11:47.135 --> 01:11:48.735
we had distributed

01:11:50.255 --> 01:11:51.695
few days ago electronically.

01:11:51.695 --> 01:11:52.655
It's also in your

01:11:53.120 --> 01:11:54.560
so I need to pick up the,

01:11:55.600 --> 01:12:02.959
Eric's been working on it. He and I discussed, my personal opinion is that it probably makes sense for him to

01:12:03.280 --> 01:12:05.680
talk about the highlights, talk about the impact,

01:12:06.315 --> 01:12:11.755
talk about any material variances from this year and what the impacts of those are gonna be.

01:12:12.635 --> 01:12:16.315
As most of you are aware, the original proposed budget

01:12:16.795 --> 01:12:18.555
came in at about 11%

01:12:18.555 --> 01:12:19.115
higher

01:12:19.720 --> 01:12:23.400
than the current budget this year, which would have meant a substantial

01:12:23.640 --> 01:12:24.920
mill increase.

01:12:26.120 --> 01:12:27.000
Obviously, that

01:12:27.480 --> 01:12:28.600
was deemed

01:12:29.400 --> 01:12:32.600
excessive by both the Board of Selectmen and the Board of Finance.

01:12:33.240 --> 01:12:34.440
We were looking for something

01:12:34.965 --> 01:12:40.965
significantly less. And now with the pandemic upon us, we are obviously looking for a lot less. And

01:12:41.605 --> 01:12:45.525
Eric, you've kind of summarized it in your document, but do you want to kind of give us your

01:12:46.245 --> 01:12:47.125
overview on that?

01:12:48.150 --> 01:12:49.270
>> Sure.

01:12:49.670 --> 01:12:50.950
I'll give you

01:12:51.350 --> 01:12:52.550
the overview.

01:12:52.550 --> 01:12:54.790
First of all, this is a budget that

01:12:55.110 --> 01:13:00.230
was largely presented to the Board of Selectmen at their April 13 meeting.

01:13:01.555 --> 01:13:08.995
And the board made some changes to it and recommended that it be moved on to the Board of Finance.

01:13:09.955 --> 01:13:12.035
The Board of Selectmen

01:13:12.355 --> 01:13:12.915
had

01:13:13.200 --> 01:13:14.480
recommended

01:13:14.480 --> 01:13:17.360
that the elementary school budget

01:13:17.520 --> 01:13:18.800
be reduced

01:13:18.800 --> 01:13:21.280
by $200,000

01:13:21.760 --> 01:13:22.560
The

01:13:23.360 --> 01:13:24.800
board of

01:13:25.200 --> 01:13:25.840
education

01:13:26.985 --> 01:13:30.425
has proposed $149,000

01:13:30.425 --> 01:13:31.305
reduction

01:13:31.385 --> 01:13:33.305
from their current budget.

01:13:34.265 --> 01:13:38.345
We do not have the final numbers from RAM yet.

01:13:38.820 --> 01:13:41.460
RAM is meeting even as we speak,

01:13:41.700 --> 01:13:47.140
but they have pushed back their adoption date. They were originally gonna pass their budget tonight.

01:13:47.380 --> 01:13:50.340
They have pushed that back to the twenty fifth.

01:13:50.580 --> 01:13:54.665
So they're going to adopt a budget in about three days.

01:13:55.065 --> 01:13:56.585
The best guess

01:13:56.665 --> 01:13:59.385
I have from the superintendent

01:13:59.385 --> 01:14:03.145
is that they are going to end up with a level budget.

01:14:03.785 --> 01:14:06.345
And a level budget from our perspective

01:14:07.180 --> 01:14:10.060
will mean about a $50,000

01:14:10.060 --> 01:14:11.100
reduction

01:14:11.580 --> 01:14:14.060
from the number that is

01:14:14.460 --> 01:14:16.460
in the budget before you.

01:14:16.700 --> 01:14:18.060
So AES

01:14:18.060 --> 01:14:18.860
is

01:14:19.100 --> 01:14:20.220
proposing

01:14:20.220 --> 01:14:21.980
$50,000

01:14:21.980 --> 01:14:22.780
less

01:14:23.075 --> 01:14:25.475
of a cut than what's in the budget,

01:14:27.235 --> 01:14:29.875
RAM will likely come out with

01:14:30.115 --> 01:14:33.395
a 52 to $53,000

01:14:33.395 --> 01:14:36.995
reduction if what their proposal is

01:14:37.235 --> 01:14:37.795
ends up.

01:14:38.870 --> 01:14:45.030
And so the net budget would be basically what's presented to you in this calculation,

01:14:45.590 --> 01:14:48.230
than slightly less from the elementary school,

01:14:48.470 --> 01:14:49.750
more from RAM.

01:14:51.110 --> 01:14:51.830
So

01:14:52.545 --> 01:14:57.105
just so that we know this budget is proposed as

01:14:58.465 --> 01:15:01.665
for lack of a better term, a crisis budget,

01:15:02.545 --> 01:15:03.905
and that we're

01:15:03.905 --> 01:15:05.360
trying to address

01:15:05.680 --> 01:15:08.480
the biggest of the town's issues

01:15:08.800 --> 01:15:11.840
and letting in underfunding

01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:14.160
the capital funds.

01:15:14.800 --> 01:15:16.080
So with

01:15:16.720 --> 01:15:19.600
this budget, we are deliberately underfunding

01:15:19.995 --> 01:15:24.475
the Public Works Capital Fund, the Fire Department Capital Fund,

01:15:24.875 --> 01:15:33.675
and the Town's Building Maintenance Fund. And we're doing that just simply because that's basically what we

01:15:33.030 --> 01:15:34.070
have to do.

01:15:34.470 --> 01:15:36.390
So we are setting aside

01:15:36.550 --> 01:15:37.270
money

01:15:37.910 --> 01:15:41.430
enough to get us through the design and engineering

01:15:41.670 --> 01:15:42.550
for

01:15:42.550 --> 01:15:43.190
the

01:15:43.990 --> 01:15:45.830
bridge over the Hop River

01:15:46.710 --> 01:15:48.070
on Bunker Hill.

01:15:49.325 --> 01:15:49.965
We

01:15:51.085 --> 01:15:55.245
are starting that process or we have started that process already.

01:15:55.565 --> 01:15:57.405
We've applied for and got

01:15:58.045 --> 01:16:04.910
the matching federal funds. So we know that we're gonna pay for about 80% of that with federal money,

01:16:05.310 --> 01:16:10.750
but our take as a town is gonna be in the $450,000

01:16:10.750 --> 01:16:11.710
category

01:16:11.790 --> 01:16:14.430
of which we're putting aside about 160

01:16:14.430 --> 01:16:15.390
now

01:16:15.390 --> 01:16:16.835
to start the the process.

01:16:17.075 --> 01:16:19.315
We're also putting some money

01:16:19.555 --> 01:16:22.755
in the bridge and culvert fund

01:16:23.715 --> 01:16:25.555
because we know we have

01:16:26.195 --> 01:16:30.355
six to seven medium sized culverts

01:16:29.560 --> 01:16:31.320
that are in disrepair.

01:16:31.640 --> 01:16:34.440
Most of them are 50 to 70

01:16:34.440 --> 01:16:35.400
years old

01:16:35.880 --> 01:16:41.640
and they're having problems. We have hired a professional engineer who specializes

01:16:42.555 --> 01:16:46.475
in that and specifically in drainage and drainage structures

01:16:46.635 --> 01:16:49.355
to give a more complete evaluation

01:16:49.515 --> 01:16:51.675
so we can kind of prioritize

01:16:51.755 --> 01:16:56.875
what we need to do. But we know there are at least two that are you know in

01:16:58.900 --> 01:17:01.620
some way down the failure mode path.

01:17:03.540 --> 01:17:05.540
In both cases,

01:17:05.780 --> 01:17:11.620
the head walls have fallen off and there's significant erosion under the metal pipes.

01:17:12.020 --> 01:17:16.145
So we're not putting aside enough money to actually do anything

01:17:16.865 --> 01:17:17.905
other than

01:17:18.145 --> 01:17:20.625
we could do some emergency

01:17:20.625 --> 01:17:29.910
shoring if we have to. We're just putting enough money to get through the design and engineering work on at least one of the critical culverts

01:17:29.910 --> 01:17:31.190
in town.

01:17:31.750 --> 01:17:32.470
So

01:17:34.870 --> 01:17:35.830
we

01:17:36.230 --> 01:17:41.190
I also want to say that one of the long term problems this town has

01:17:41.405 --> 01:17:43.245
is we spend

01:17:43.485 --> 01:17:47.405
on the municipal budget side, in other words, non education,

01:17:47.565 --> 01:17:49.005
about 30%

01:17:49.005 --> 01:17:50.525
less per capita

01:17:50.525 --> 01:17:51.885
than our peers.

01:17:53.085 --> 01:17:55.325
And that overall

01:17:55.720 --> 01:17:57.320
little funding level

01:17:57.480 --> 01:18:00.600
has really caused an infrastructure deteriorate

01:18:00.760 --> 01:18:03.560
and we're going to have to address it this year.

01:18:04.840 --> 01:18:05.560
So

01:18:05.640 --> 01:18:13.745
right now what I'm trying to do is this budget is essentially a maintenance budget. In other words, the roads and the buildings

01:18:14.465 --> 01:18:17.185
will not get any worse, hopefully,

01:18:18.225 --> 01:18:23.185
but we're not really chipping away at the backlog of work that we need to do.

01:18:23.980 --> 01:18:30.060
So we have some really good things going for us. The first is we have very little bond obligation.

01:18:30.380 --> 01:18:34.540
We have a little bit of a bond note for RAM, our portion of the RAM.

01:18:35.820 --> 01:18:40.455
There's still a bond for that. And there is a bond for AES

01:18:40.775 --> 01:18:45.495
that will be cleared in either one more year or two more years.

01:18:46.615 --> 01:18:48.615
We don't have a lot of debt

01:18:48.775 --> 01:18:51.895
and we have a reasonable capital reserve

01:18:52.420 --> 01:18:59.060
and we probably have enough capital reserve that we're not going to need to worry about short term borrowing

01:18:59.300 --> 01:19:00.100
if

01:19:00.100 --> 01:19:01.620
we have

01:19:01.860 --> 01:19:04.100
a significant delay

01:19:04.180 --> 01:19:06.420
in people paying their taxes either,

01:19:07.315 --> 01:19:09.635
definitely not with the April quarter,

01:19:09.955 --> 01:19:15.075
but even the first quarter of the year, which we take in a significant fraction of our yearly budget.

01:19:15.315 --> 01:19:21.795
If that's delayed, we have enough of a cushion that we should be able to get through that without

01:19:21.450 --> 01:19:24.410
having to take out any kind of tax anticipation

01:19:24.810 --> 01:19:25.450
note.

01:19:25.690 --> 01:19:27.290
So that's really good.

01:19:27.690 --> 01:19:30.010
Not all towns are in that position.

01:19:30.810 --> 01:19:33.290
So the other thing is that

01:19:33.735 --> 01:19:35.815
we are repurposing

01:19:36.055 --> 01:19:37.335
every fund

01:19:37.575 --> 01:19:39.895
that we're not actively

01:19:39.975 --> 01:19:41.495
planning on using.

01:19:41.895 --> 01:19:45.895
So that includes the Public Works Building Improvement Fund,

01:19:46.550 --> 01:19:48.790
the new library fund,

01:19:49.110 --> 01:19:51.430
the AES expansion fund,

01:19:51.910 --> 01:19:53.750
and the money that is still

01:19:54.230 --> 01:19:56.470
in the Times Farm Bridge

01:19:57.350 --> 01:19:59.030
Capital Project Fund

01:19:59.285 --> 01:20:02.645
minus the money we still owe for retainage

01:20:02.645 --> 01:20:04.165
for the contractor

01:20:04.245 --> 01:20:06.805
as we try to finish closing that out.

01:20:07.125 --> 01:20:08.885
So basically what we're doing,

01:20:09.685 --> 01:20:14.965
we can do things a little differently this year just simply because of the governor's directives.

01:20:15.430 --> 01:20:19.270
And what I'm suggesting is just take all those unexpended

01:20:19.270 --> 01:20:19.990
funds

01:20:20.310 --> 01:20:22.390
as well as the 500,

01:20:22.390 --> 01:20:24.149
well not 500,000,

01:20:24.150 --> 01:20:27.109
the $415,000

01:20:27.270 --> 01:20:29.590
from the unexpended fund balance

01:20:30.015 --> 01:20:33.775
and transfer all of that to the general fund

01:20:33.855 --> 01:20:39.455
in next year's budget and then just break out our spending priorities directly.

01:20:39.455 --> 01:20:51.820
Instead of trying to say, well, some of this fund will go to this fund, some of this fund will go here. We're just dumping everything into the general fund and then funding the most important things out of that.

01:20:53.420 --> 01:20:56.380
So one of the implications of this budget,

01:20:56.540 --> 01:20:59.180
and I want you all to be aware of it because this is

01:20:59.865 --> 01:21:03.225
a departure from what we've done in the past

01:21:03.625 --> 01:21:05.785
is that we are

01:21:06.025 --> 01:21:07.065
using

01:21:07.065 --> 01:21:08.905
some of fund balance

01:21:09.225 --> 01:21:10.345
for what is

01:21:10.905 --> 01:21:12.825
really operating expenses.

01:21:13.580 --> 01:21:19.820
And what I would call operating expenses is the money we spend the roadwork money

01:21:19.980 --> 01:21:22.300
because the reality is the town

01:21:22.460 --> 01:21:26.700
year in year out needs to spend about $400,000

01:21:27.295 --> 01:21:30.815
just in paving and pavement preservation.

01:21:31.375 --> 01:21:36.254
And we're going, at least for this year, we're going to be funding that

01:21:36.735 --> 01:21:40.495
from the unexpended fund balance essentially.

01:21:40.815 --> 01:21:41.775
So we are,

01:21:44.560 --> 01:21:49.040
realistically we are using some fund balance money for operating

01:21:49.040 --> 01:21:49.760
expenses.

01:21:50.080 --> 01:21:53.840
And I wanna be clear upfront that that's what's happening.

01:21:54.800 --> 01:21:56.000
This budget does

01:21:56.605 --> 01:21:57.485
anticipate

01:21:57.485 --> 01:21:59.725
a series of personnel changes.

01:22:00.445 --> 01:22:06.125
Probably the most important is the fourth full time public works employee.

01:22:06.925 --> 01:22:13.920
As many know, at one point, the town of Andover had a five person full time staff

01:22:14.160 --> 01:22:17.440
and two more full time summer staff

01:22:17.600 --> 01:22:20.240
during the paving and construction

01:22:20.240 --> 01:22:21.040
season.

01:22:21.440 --> 01:22:24.160
We've trimmed that number down to

01:22:24.325 --> 01:22:27.045
less than half the numbers of man hours

01:22:27.205 --> 01:22:30.725
that the town used to have available for the public crew.

01:22:31.045 --> 01:22:35.925
And we've cut it down to the point where our backlog of drainage and projects

01:22:36.565 --> 01:22:37.845
has gotten really long.

01:22:38.510 --> 01:22:40.270
Looking at it conservatively,

01:22:40.670 --> 01:22:45.710
right now we have a seven to ten year backlog of drainage projects that need to get done.

01:22:46.030 --> 01:22:48.110
So adding a fourth person

01:22:48.270 --> 01:22:51.230
will help us bring that backlog down

01:22:51.390 --> 01:22:52.110
over time.

01:22:54.285 --> 01:23:00.365
It's not going be an instant fix, but it's something the towns really needed to do for quite a while.

01:23:01.085 --> 01:23:03.725
We are anticipating adding

01:23:03.885 --> 01:23:07.005
a part time senior services coordinator.

01:23:08.170 --> 01:23:11.370
We currently have a senior services coordinator,

01:23:11.610 --> 01:23:12.890
but that responsibility

01:23:12.890 --> 01:23:15.450
is given to another town employee,

01:23:15.690 --> 01:23:17.450
but we really don't have

01:23:18.010 --> 01:23:18.730
the

01:23:21.290 --> 01:23:22.170
time

01:23:22.725 --> 01:23:26.005
allotted to do a reasonable job of that.

01:23:26.325 --> 01:23:30.084
And our overall funding for senior services in general

01:23:30.805 --> 01:23:32.965
is roughly what we fund

01:23:33.205 --> 01:23:36.805
for one and a half elementary school

01:23:36.420 --> 01:23:40.900
students is what we provide to all senior services collectively.

01:23:41.380 --> 01:23:43.940
So it's a pretty small budget overall.

01:23:44.340 --> 01:23:49.060
We're talking about increasing that, but it's still a pretty reasonable expense.

01:23:50.305 --> 01:23:53.185
We're having a real hard time keeping up

01:23:53.505 --> 01:23:59.425
at both the zoning enforcement officer and the inland wetlands officer positions.

01:24:00.545 --> 01:24:02.865
We're way understaffed

01:24:03.025 --> 01:24:03.825
time wise.

01:24:04.650 --> 01:24:08.490
What happened was our longtime person at that position

01:24:08.730 --> 01:24:12.330
had been just putting in however many hours it took,

01:24:12.490 --> 01:24:14.730
even though he wasn't getting paid for it.

01:24:15.450 --> 01:24:17.770
And so we've been understaffing that position

01:24:18.635 --> 01:24:19.915
for many years

01:24:20.315 --> 01:24:21.595
and we

01:24:21.995 --> 01:24:25.115
desperately need to address it because we're

01:24:25.115 --> 01:24:27.835
A, getting an awful lot of complaints about that.

01:24:28.555 --> 01:24:30.875
And B, we're not getting enough

01:24:31.515 --> 01:24:32.635
through to,

01:24:33.320 --> 01:24:36.600
we need their services for town projects also.

01:24:36.840 --> 01:24:37.960
And right now

01:24:38.440 --> 01:24:40.120
that's

01:24:40.120 --> 01:24:41.400
being problematic.

01:24:42.920 --> 01:24:44.680
So the next thing

01:24:44.920 --> 01:24:47.400
is increasing the compensation

01:24:47.400 --> 01:24:49.320
for my administrative

01:24:49.215 --> 01:24:49.695
assistant.

01:24:52.415 --> 01:24:54.655
Is woefully underpaid

01:24:54.735 --> 01:24:59.294
based on her job description and what she actually does for the town.

01:24:59.775 --> 01:25:03.455
And I would also like to bump her up right up to nineteen hours

01:25:03.820 --> 01:25:05.820
which is the maximum,

01:25:05.820 --> 01:25:08.620
you know, we can do as a part time person,

01:25:09.420 --> 01:25:10.380
without

01:25:10.380 --> 01:25:11.420
benefits.

01:25:14.300 --> 01:25:16.940
Let's see, at the same time we are decreasing

01:25:17.545 --> 01:25:20.024
the hours for the tax collector

01:25:20.345 --> 01:25:21.225
back

01:25:21.225 --> 01:25:24.584
to a part time position, which it was historically.

01:25:24.905 --> 01:25:28.185
That is still going to require a union negotiation

01:25:28.185 --> 01:25:30.905
as soon as you actually approve that,

01:25:32.220 --> 01:25:33.980
presuming you do approve that.

01:25:34.460 --> 01:25:35.100
And

01:25:35.340 --> 01:25:37.420
as we've talked about before,

01:25:37.420 --> 01:25:38.220
we are

01:25:38.380 --> 01:25:39.260
planning

01:25:39.260 --> 01:25:44.265
on combining the budget departments for the town hall and the elementary schools.

01:25:44.825 --> 01:25:46.185
Other things,

01:25:46.425 --> 01:25:49.705
this budget includes a 2.5%

01:25:49.705 --> 01:25:50.505
raise

01:25:50.585 --> 01:25:52.265
for town employees,

01:25:52.825 --> 01:25:56.745
which was voted on and passed by the board of selectmen.

01:25:57.065 --> 01:26:00.640
And they are the body that determines that.

01:26:01.760 --> 01:26:05.280
And that doesn't take into account the union employees

01:26:05.440 --> 01:26:08.160
whose raises are per contract.

01:26:08.960 --> 01:26:09.680
So

01:26:11.040 --> 01:26:15.120
the other thing that's gone up a fair bit is the benefits.

01:26:17.455 --> 01:26:21.055
Although the health care costs premiums

01:26:21.135 --> 01:26:22.895
did not go up that much,

01:26:23.135 --> 01:26:24.015
MRF,

01:26:24.335 --> 01:26:25.615
which is the retirement

01:26:26.095 --> 01:26:29.775
program, did go up somewhat, not a great deal but somewhat.

01:26:30.640 --> 01:26:34.560
But the major expense for that is the cost of adding

01:26:34.720 --> 01:26:36.000
a fourth

01:26:36.000 --> 01:26:37.840
public works employee.

01:26:38.320 --> 01:26:39.600
And one of our

01:26:40.720 --> 01:26:42.080
real unknowns

01:26:42.320 --> 01:26:44.320
is that we will be replacing

01:26:45.195 --> 01:26:45.835
the,

01:26:47.675 --> 01:26:49.355
the person who,

01:26:50.715 --> 01:26:54.475
the foreman for public works, Ed, will retire.

01:26:54.955 --> 01:26:57.035
He's slated to retire in November.

01:26:57.790 --> 01:27:06.030
And so we're going to have two positions for which we don't know what our benefit exposure is going to be because there'll be full time positions

01:27:06.350 --> 01:27:10.590
and depending on whether they're single or they have families or they're married,

01:27:11.390 --> 01:27:12.030
our costs,

01:27:12.635 --> 01:27:15.995
the variability in that cost line is 30

01:27:16.475 --> 01:27:18.075
plus thousand dollars

01:27:18.155 --> 01:27:23.355
based on depending on whether they're single or whether they're married with dependents.

01:27:24.395 --> 01:27:27.115
So we chose kind of an intermediate value

01:27:27.540 --> 01:27:28.500
for that.

01:27:30.260 --> 01:27:33.300
So we could be high or low, 10

01:27:33.620 --> 01:27:35.860
plus thousand dollars on that.

01:27:36.900 --> 01:27:38.100
So again,

01:27:38.260 --> 01:27:39.460
this budget

01:27:39.540 --> 01:27:40.820
is designed

01:27:43.455 --> 01:27:45.055
to stop the bleeding

01:27:45.135 --> 01:27:47.215
in the sense that we're

01:27:47.215 --> 01:27:56.620
funding enough of the maintenance to get the things we absolutely have to do, but we're not really knocking down much of our backlog.

01:27:56.940 --> 01:27:58.460
And I want to be honest,

01:27:59.100 --> 01:28:02.700
in that a lot of these things we're deferring spending on,

01:28:03.100 --> 01:28:05.900
we are going to end up spending that money.

01:28:06.060 --> 01:28:08.700
We're just not spending it this year.

01:28:09.260 --> 01:28:12.584
So, you know, we took a bunch out of the capital,

01:28:13.305 --> 01:28:15.145
which is appropriate in this

01:28:16.025 --> 01:28:17.624
time of crisis. But,

01:28:18.185 --> 01:28:20.345
you know, I hate to say it, but we

01:28:20.745 --> 01:28:24.425
are going to spend a lot of that at some point

01:28:24.665 --> 01:28:25.465
in the future.

01:28:27.410 --> 01:28:33.249
The senior center, we took out all the additional funding for the senior center.

01:28:33.810 --> 01:28:34.770
There is

01:28:35.090 --> 01:28:37.330
money in the account right now.

01:28:38.295 --> 01:28:44.935
So we have enough money that the committee can explore design options

01:28:45.335 --> 01:28:49.815
and get through basically the engineering architecture and design

01:28:50.250 --> 01:28:51.930
for a new senior center.

01:28:52.650 --> 01:28:56.810
But we certainly don't have enough money in it to actually

01:28:56.970 --> 01:28:58.730
fully build one.

01:29:00.410 --> 01:29:03.850
That's what we didn't add anything to that this year.

01:29:05.315 --> 01:29:07.955
The other big one is roadwork.

01:29:08.195 --> 01:29:12.035
If you're all curious, you can see the roadwork plan

01:29:12.275 --> 01:29:14.435
on the town website.

01:29:14.995 --> 01:29:18.195
I put that on about ten days ago or so.

01:29:18.595 --> 01:29:19.075
Basically,

01:29:19.520 --> 01:29:24.000
the goal with the roadwork plan at this point is to

01:29:24.080 --> 01:29:26.000
fund what we can

01:29:26.160 --> 01:29:28.560
to prevent any deterioration.

01:29:29.520 --> 01:29:36.745
So we have shifted our budget priorities to be majority pavement preservation.

01:29:36.985 --> 01:29:41.625
In other words, do everything we can to keep the good pavement good,

01:29:41.945 --> 01:29:47.305
but we're accepting the fact that we're probably not going to be chipping away at much of our backlog

01:29:47.390 --> 01:29:49.870
of bad sections of roads

01:29:49.950 --> 01:29:52.110
in the upcoming budget.

01:29:54.030 --> 01:30:00.830
The other limiting factor right now is our ability to do the drainage work that has to be done prior to

01:30:01.375 --> 01:30:03.535
paving some of the bad areas.

01:30:03.615 --> 01:30:09.775
Because the reality is most of the bad sections are bad because the underlying substrate

01:30:09.935 --> 01:30:11.455
stays

01:30:12.495 --> 01:30:15.540
excessively wet and we're getting failure

01:30:16.420 --> 01:30:17.060
from.

01:30:18.420 --> 01:30:20.820
>> Part of what we're doing is trying to address

01:30:22.100 --> 01:30:26.820
by adding, by prioritizing adding the fourth public works person,

01:30:27.060 --> 01:30:28.500
we're hoping to start addressing

01:30:29.085 --> 01:30:36.045
the drainage issues to the extent we can now. So when we finally have the budget to start repaving,

01:30:36.605 --> 01:30:40.125
we have that, you know, we're further along with that.

01:30:41.405 --> 01:30:44.125
As far as permanent funds go,

01:30:45.110 --> 01:30:48.790
one of the things I did last year was I looked at

01:30:49.030 --> 01:30:50.710
the fire department.

01:30:50.790 --> 01:30:56.790
Well, actually they had a budget, so I didn't really have to do much for them, but the public works equipment,

01:30:57.590 --> 01:30:59.430
and then building maintenance.

01:31:00.185 --> 01:31:02.665
And what I did was put together

01:31:03.065 --> 01:31:04.025
basically

01:31:04.025 --> 01:31:05.225
based on

01:31:05.945 --> 01:31:08.265
standard equipment profiles for

01:31:08.665 --> 01:31:15.145
the town and for the fire department, how much the town needed to budget per year for those funds.

01:31:16.160 --> 01:31:27.440
And so what we've done in this budget is we funded them essentially at half of what we should fund them, which is more than we funded them in the past. So it's an improvement,

01:31:27.840 --> 01:31:29.280
but it's acknowledging

01:31:29.280 --> 01:31:35.835
that it's not really what we should be putting aside this year for capital equipment.

01:31:36.555 --> 01:31:39.835
But at least it gets us somewhere and it's a reasonable,

01:31:40.075 --> 01:31:41.195
it's a compromise.

01:31:44.190 --> 01:31:44.910
So

01:31:46.270 --> 01:31:47.150
that's

01:31:47.310 --> 01:31:49.470
from like 50,000

01:31:49.470 --> 01:31:50.670
foot view.

01:31:52.030 --> 01:31:54.430
I wish I had better news for you.

01:31:54.910 --> 01:31:58.910
I will say we're doing a lot better than a lot of towns.

01:31:59.875 --> 01:32:01.795
We have very little debt.

01:32:02.195 --> 01:32:05.715
We have always run this town pretty frugally.

01:32:06.195 --> 01:32:08.675
So we're

01:32:08.675 --> 01:32:09.874
just basically

01:32:10.435 --> 01:32:11.155
continuing

01:32:12.060 --> 01:32:13.499
down that path,

01:32:14.620 --> 01:32:17.499
but we are putting off some capital spending

01:32:17.660 --> 01:32:18.300
that

01:32:19.100 --> 01:32:19.500
will

01:32:21.180 --> 01:32:23.340
I guess what I would say is I'm

01:32:23.500 --> 01:32:28.435
trying not put off any capital spending if it will cost us significantly

01:32:28.435 --> 01:32:29.155
more

01:32:29.315 --> 01:32:31.555
to put it off than to do it now,

01:32:32.035 --> 01:32:33.315
and kind of prioritizing

01:32:33.315 --> 01:32:34.355
it that way.

01:32:35.635 --> 01:32:37.555
That's it from the overview,

01:32:37.635 --> 01:32:39.795
unless you have specific

01:32:39.570 --> 01:32:40.770
questions for me.

01:32:41.330 --> 01:32:42.530
>> Before

01:32:42.530 --> 01:32:46.370
anyone else jumps in here, I just wanna point out a couple other things.

01:32:47.170 --> 01:32:48.850
The board of selectmen had

01:32:49.490 --> 01:32:50.530
much debate

01:32:50.690 --> 01:32:51.970
on the budget as a whole.

01:32:53.135 --> 01:32:57.855
The biggest thing that we were looking for Eric to do and made adjustments to

01:32:58.335 --> 01:33:00.895
was to not be artificially

01:33:00.975 --> 01:33:02.655
lowering the tax rate

01:33:02.975 --> 01:33:05.375
by funding ongoing operations

01:33:05.375 --> 01:33:06.175
with reserves.

01:33:06.870 --> 01:33:17.350
So we tweaked to the budget that he'd originally given us to to make sure that was taken into account. So the operating budget that we'll be working off of is funded

01:33:18.310 --> 01:33:20.070
by taxation,

01:33:20.310 --> 01:33:20.870
not reserves.

01:33:21.435 --> 01:33:23.834
So if something changes next year,

01:33:24.315 --> 01:33:29.035
it means we won't be able to fund capital funds or we won't be able to do any capital projects,

01:33:29.515 --> 01:33:30.315
but the functional

01:33:31.035 --> 01:33:36.235
part of the budget that includes staffing and services provided

01:33:35.720 --> 01:33:37.960
is funded via the current

01:33:38.840 --> 01:33:43.400
mill rate taxation that we're asking for. So it's basically flat across the board,

01:33:44.200 --> 01:33:46.680
but it is encompassed in there. So we're

01:33:47.160 --> 01:33:49.080
not looking at like a fake number. Do you know what I mean?

01:33:50.985 --> 01:33:58.825
>> Adrian, just to jump in for a second. I mean, there's a little bit of a semantic issue there because to an extent the town roadwork,

01:33:58.825 --> 01:34:00.105
which is under capital,

01:34:00.265 --> 01:34:02.905
I mean, I think this is what Eric was getting at before.

01:34:04.025 --> 01:34:10.470
It's under capital. So in a sense you're right we're not actually taking fund balance and

01:34:11.430 --> 01:34:12.310
funding

01:34:12.470 --> 01:34:26.655
day to day operations off of that which is a bad move. I think we all agree on that but at the same time if the projection is we need to spend X every year on the roads and it just happens to flow through capital and we take that money from the fund balance,

01:34:26.815 --> 01:34:27.455
it

01:34:27.855 --> 01:34:28.735
>> kind of is.

01:34:29.615 --> 01:34:32.015
So it's really kind of how you look at it.

01:34:32.800 --> 01:34:34.480
Eric, I had a question for you

01:34:34.800 --> 01:34:35.840
just to

01:34:36.080 --> 01:34:40.000
get back to the slash and burn that we've done on the capital budget.

01:34:40.160 --> 01:34:42.320
To the extent that there was one item,

01:34:43.840 --> 01:34:47.440
just listening to your rundown and the presentations you've given us before,

01:34:48.165 --> 01:34:53.524
it seems to me the one item that is probably the most critical that if we wanted to look at

01:34:54.405 --> 01:34:56.805
more closely is the culvert work.

01:34:57.205 --> 01:34:58.405
Would you agree with that?

01:34:59.445 --> 01:35:00.645
The drainage and culverts?

01:35:02.710 --> 01:35:05.270
In terms of what? In terms of

01:35:05.910 --> 01:35:06.630
In

01:35:07.190 --> 01:35:09.190
>> terms of trying not to kick the can too much?

01:35:10.790 --> 01:35:15.190
>> So here so we ran into one snag this year

01:35:16.245 --> 01:35:21.925
And our first snag was the engineer that we hired to do this broke his neck

01:35:22.325 --> 01:35:23.365
this spring

01:35:23.765 --> 01:35:25.685
and that's delayed.

01:35:25.685 --> 01:35:33.340
I had anticipated by this time I would have a full blown culvert replacement plan to present to you.

01:35:34.300 --> 01:35:35.340
I do not.

01:35:36.220 --> 01:35:38.700
That's delayed at least three months.

01:35:39.180 --> 01:35:39.980
Fortunately,

01:35:40.140 --> 01:35:42.635
he is back in operation

01:35:42.875 --> 01:35:45.355
and he is actively working on that,

01:35:45.675 --> 01:35:49.435
but we were not where we need to be.

01:35:50.635 --> 01:35:52.715
My guess is that

01:35:53.035 --> 01:36:01.930
just looking at the smaller culvert work we're going to do this year and the drainage work we're going to end up doing this year,

01:36:02.730 --> 01:36:03.370
we're

01:36:04.330 --> 01:36:06.410
real in looking at permitting

01:36:06.650 --> 01:36:07.770
realistically,

01:36:07.930 --> 01:36:10.410
we're probably not doing it

01:36:10.585 --> 01:36:12.425
this year anyway.

01:36:12.985 --> 01:36:13.544
So

01:36:15.225 --> 01:36:17.465
I'm fairly comfortable that

01:36:17.945 --> 01:36:24.105
what I wanted to make sure is that we have enough money to get completely through the design and engineering

01:36:24.570 --> 01:36:27.290
for our biggest priority small,

01:36:27.290 --> 01:36:29.690
you know, medium sized culvert,

01:36:31.210 --> 01:36:38.010
you know, by next year with the intention of funding one every year or every other year.

01:36:38.785 --> 01:36:41.505
So I think we're actually okay.

01:36:42.065 --> 01:36:45.185
I mean would I like more money in that budget? Sure.

01:36:45.665 --> 01:36:47.745
But I think I can live with it.

01:36:48.465 --> 01:36:53.105
>> Okay so it's really almost a timing thing. Is this something we're really going to have to look at next budget cycle?

01:36:54.520 --> 01:36:55.240
Correct.

01:36:55.400 --> 01:36:56.600
Okay. Yeah.

01:36:57.800 --> 01:36:58.680
>> To your point,

01:36:59.880 --> 01:37:02.280
Rob, yeah, it is a semantic issue.

01:37:02.520 --> 01:37:03.560
You're you're correct.

01:37:03.720 --> 01:37:07.080
We do so we do need to do that. But I I think part of our concern is

01:37:07.560 --> 01:37:09.720
we also don't wanna be in a position where we

01:37:10.325 --> 01:37:16.885
jump the gun, we get a bunch out the gate, and then we stall out. So we're trying to look at a bunch of different possibilities.

01:37:17.765 --> 01:37:23.925
Once this audit comes in and we're done and that's settled, we're gonna sit down and try to look at bonding options

01:37:24.780 --> 01:37:27.100
that we have, whether it's,

01:37:28.060 --> 01:37:32.780
you know, internal bonding where we actually borrow against our reserves or

01:37:33.020 --> 01:37:35.820
outside notes and come up with a long term plan

01:37:36.460 --> 01:37:44.315
that would encompass both culverts and roads and basically try to do everything at once. We need to get a little more detail on that and and put together a package

01:37:44.555 --> 01:37:57.149
with everything going on. We felt it was most appropriate to sort of you know, we weren't ready quite ready for that either given the fact that we don't have a culvert plan in place, but once we have that back and we have our bonding answers,

01:37:57.710 --> 01:37:59.149
our plan is to basically

01:37:59.390 --> 01:38:04.749
come back to you next year with either a long term plan and a payment schedule

01:38:05.230 --> 01:38:08.975
that we would need from a tax base or a bond situation

01:38:09.295 --> 01:38:10.495
and options

01:38:10.495 --> 01:38:14.175
so that we can address that. But the key to making that happen

01:38:14.335 --> 01:38:16.735
is to have that fourth person in public works

01:38:17.535 --> 01:38:19.295
so that we can pass

01:38:19.295 --> 01:38:26.440
on the knowledge that Ed has gained all of this time to another person and then when he retires to replace him obviously,

01:38:26.600 --> 01:38:27.800
but having those four

01:38:28.280 --> 01:38:37.795
guys in place because that allows us to have a dedicated crew of two guys just doing road work that don't get pulled to go down a transfer station and crush stuff,

01:38:38.035 --> 01:38:40.595
that don't get pulled to go down to the soccer or the long

01:38:41.075 --> 01:38:41.955
hill field,

01:38:42.115 --> 01:38:44.755
that don't get pulled into town hall to move furniture.

01:38:44.915 --> 01:38:46.595
They're just going to be dedicated

01:38:46.915 --> 01:38:47.795
public works

01:38:49.710 --> 01:38:53.630
doing road stuff. So doing side reinforcements after paving,

01:38:53.950 --> 01:38:54.510
doing

01:38:54.910 --> 01:38:58.110
skimming, doing sweeping before road projects,

01:38:58.430 --> 01:39:03.470
doing culvert works, doing basin work like we had to do that emergency repair on the Wells Road.

01:39:04.955 --> 01:39:07.274
Diane, we got another participant over there.

01:39:08.715 --> 01:39:09.355
Yeah,

01:39:09.675 --> 01:39:15.595
>> so Rob's like staring at the cat. Could tell him. The cat's killing me. I'm sorry, I lost you completely.

01:39:16.340 --> 01:39:24.820
>> All he sees is a cat in a window. Fairly more interesting than your rage. I knew I lost him when I saw the cat hop up. But no, I guess our point is that

01:39:25.220 --> 01:39:28.420
that fourth guy is so important to making this plan come together

01:39:28.755 --> 01:39:34.915
so that way we can dedicate. The more we can bring in house on the road work, better off we're going to be. But we're

01:39:35.075 --> 01:39:39.235
not in a position to bring in a paver and a paving crew and all that. It's just not practical.

01:39:40.035 --> 01:39:42.995
But we are in a position to start doing a lot of the prep work,

01:39:43.315 --> 01:39:54.960
the basin work, the drainage work, so that way we're just paying for paving and we're just paying for resealing and chip sealing and microceiling and all the pavement preservation program that we need to do. So that's why we're

01:39:55.280 --> 01:39:56.560
a little behind on

01:39:56.975 --> 01:40:01.055
the total package Rob and we'd like to be a little further along but we're

01:40:01.615 --> 01:40:05.215
trying to tackle as much as we can without that we can actually accomplish.

01:40:05.775 --> 01:40:07.215
Understood, thanks Adrian.

01:40:15.480 --> 01:40:16.280
Okay.

01:40:17.000 --> 01:40:19.320
Any other questions or comments?

01:40:23.160 --> 01:40:24.200
>> For Eric?

01:40:24.600 --> 01:40:30.475
Eric, it's Kurt. Correct me if I'm wrong. Are we looking at on the town budget a

01:40:30.875 --> 01:40:31.755
2.5

01:40:31.755 --> 01:40:33.755
mil increase in the budget?

01:40:36.075 --> 01:40:38.875
No. You guys? Looks like two.

01:40:39.435 --> 01:40:40.955
>> So we are looking at a

01:40:41.890 --> 01:40:43.970
2.0%

01:40:44.850 --> 01:40:46.450
mill rate increase.

01:40:46.450 --> 01:40:47.970
The estimated

01:40:47.970 --> 01:40:49.010
mill rate

01:40:49.330 --> 01:40:50.610
currently

01:40:50.770 --> 01:40:53.170
will increase by 0.7

